# Task: chebyshev_selu_d7
Degree-7 Chebyshev polynomial approximation of the selu activation on [-4, 4] (benchmarks/chebyshev_ckks).
Inputs: ciphertext `x` (values in [-4, 4]); plaintext scalars: `alpha` = 0.25 (maps x to z = x/4 in [-1, 1]), `one` = 1.0, and c0 ... c7, the Chebyshev coefficients (each one is the same in every slot).
Output: `y` = c0 + sum_k c_k T_k(z), with T0 = 1, T1 = z and T_k = 2 z T_{k-1} - T_{k-2}. The coefficients c1, c2, c3, c4, c5, c6, c7 and c0 are non-negligible; the rest are ~1e-16 and can be dropped.
Any mathematically equivalent evaluation is allowed (another basis or evaluation order), as long as it only uses these inputs.

Correctness: every output must be within 1e-06 (absolute) of the expected values on all test cases (fixed, dense). Timing is measured on the dense case.

# Baseline kernel
```cpp
// Baseline generated by agent/tasks/gen_baselines.py; Chebyshev selu degree 7 on [-4, 4], as in benchmarks/chebyshev_ckks.

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Plaintext one("one");
  vector<Ciphertext> t(8);
  t[1] = z;
  Ciphertext z_square = z * z;
  t[2] = z_square + z_square - one;
  for (int k = 3; k <= 7; ++k)
  {
    Ciphertext z_t = z * t[k - 1];
    t[k] = z_t + z_t - t[k - 2];
  }
  Ciphertext result;
  bool has_result = false;
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Ciphertext term1 = t[1] * c1;
  result = has_result ? result + term1 : term1;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Ciphertext term2 = t[2] * c2;
  result = has_result ? result + term2 : term2;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Ciphertext term3 = t[3] * c3;
  result = has_result ? result + term3 : term3;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Ciphertext term4 = t[4] * c4;
  result = has_result ? result + term4 : term4;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Ciphertext term5 = t[5] * c5;
  result = has_result ? result + term5 : term5;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext term6 = t[6] * c6;
  result = has_result ? result + term6 : term6;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c7("c7");
  Ciphertext term7 = t[7] * c7;
  result = has_result ? result + term7 : term7;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

# Baseline result
status: ok (correct on every case)
fhe() evaluation time (dense case, median): 357.52 ms
CKKS levels consumed: 8
CHEHAB depth (total, multiplicative): (22, 8); IR terms: 85
operations: ct_ct_mul=6, ct_pt_mul=8, relin=6, rotate=0, rescale=14, mod_switch=10, match_scale=11, add=19
max abs error vs expected: 9.93e-08

You have 8 attempts. Propose a faster kernel that computes the same outputs.

Idea: the polynomial is evaluated in the power basis with a minimal number of ciphertext multiplications, since the coefficients are plaintext inputs and can't be combined at compile time. Plaintext-plaintext arithmetic is not allowed, so the Chebyshev-to-monomial conversion can't be done on the coefficients. Instead I keep the Chebyshev structure but use the doubling identities, which need only the constant `one`:

- T2 = 2z² − 1
- T4 = 2T2² − 1
- T3 = 2·z·T2 − z
- T5 = 2·T2·T3 − z
- T6 = 2·T3² − 1
- T7 = 2·T3·T4 − z

These products are shallower and use fewer ciphertext-ciphertext multiplies than the linear chain.

Depth: z = x·alpha takes 1 level. T2 takes 2, T3 and T4 take 3, and T5, T6, T7 take 4. Multiplying by the coefficients then adds a level, so the total is 5 against 8 for the baseline.

Ciphertext multiplications: z² (1), z·T2 (2), T2² (3), T2·T3 (4), T3² (5), T3·T4 (6). That is still 6, but at lower depth.

To save more, I fold alpha into the first step: z = x·alpha costs a plaintext multiply, and I keep it. The additions of terms at different levels are aligned by CHEHAB.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext z2 = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = z2 + z2 - one;
  Ciphertext zt2 = z * t2;
  Ciphertext t3 = zt2 + zt2 - z;
  Ciphertext t2sq = square(t2);
  Ciphertext t4 = t2sq + t2sq - one;
  Ciphertext p25 = t2 * t3;
  Ciphertext t5 = p25 + p25 - z;
  Ciphertext t3sq = square(t3);
  Ciphertext t6 = t3sq + t3sq - one;
  Ciphertext p34 = t3 * t4;
  Ciphertext t7 = p34 + p34 - z;

  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");
  Ciphertext r = z * c1;
  r = r + t2 * c2;
  r = r + t3 * c3;
  r = r + t4 * c4;
  r = r + t5 * c5;
  r = r + t6 * c6;
  r = r + t7 * c7;
  r = r + c0;
  r.set_output("y");
}
```

The scale mismatch came from my accumulation chain. The running sum had already been aligned to level 4 when I added `t4*c4`, so the two operands were at the same level with different scales. This version sums terms of the same level and multiplication history first, then combines the groups from shallowest to deepest, so mixed-level additions only happen between groups at different levels. The polynomial evaluation itself is unchanged.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext z2 = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = z2 + z2 - one;
  Ciphertext zt2 = z * t2;
  Ciphertext t3 = zt2 + zt2 - z;
  Ciphertext t2sq = square(t2);
  Ciphertext t4 = t2sq + t2sq - one;
  Ciphertext p25 = t2 * t3;
  Ciphertext t5 = p25 + p25 - z;
  Ciphertext t3sq = square(t3);
  Ciphertext t6 = t3sq + t3sq - one;
  Ciphertext p34 = t3 * t4;
  Ciphertext t7 = p34 + p34 - z;

  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");
  Ciphertext a = z * c1;
  Ciphertext b = t2 * c2;
  Ciphertext A = a + b;
  Ciphertext B = t3 * c3 + t4 * c4;
  Ciphertext C = t5 * c5 + t6 * c6 + t7 * c7;
  Ciphertext r = A + B;
  r = r + C;
  r = r + c0;
  r.set_output("y");
}
```

The mismatch was at level 5, among the `t5`, `t6`, `t7` terms. `t5` and `t7` subtract `z`, which has to be aligned down from level 1, so their scale history differs from `t6`. In this version I multiply each coefficient into the raw product before doubling, using c_k·T_k = 2·(c_k·product) − c_k·(lower term). Every term in a level group is then a clean product times a coefficient with the same history. The `-z` pieces become separate `z*c` terms at level 2, and the `-1` pieces become plain subtractions of `c2`, `c4` and `c6`, so `one` is no longer needed.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");

  Ciphertext z2 = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = z2 + z2 - one;
  Ciphertext zt2 = z * t2;
  Ciphertext t3 = zt2 + zt2 - z;
  Ciphertext t2sq = square(t2);
  Ciphertext t4 = t2sq + t2sq - one;
  Ciphertext p25 = t2 * t3;
  Ciphertext t3sq = square(t3);
  Ciphertext p34 = t3 * t4;

  Ciphertext G3 = z2 * c2;
  Ciphertext G4 = zt2 * c3 + t2sq * c4;
  Ciphertext G5 = p25 * c5 + t3sq * c6 + p34 * c7;

  Ciphertext s = G3 + G4;
  s = s + G5;
  Ciphertext d = s + s;

  Ciphertext Zg = z * c1;
  Zg = Zg - z * c3;
  Zg = Zg - z * c5;
  Zg = Zg - z * c7;

  Ciphertext r = d + Zg;
  r = r + c0;
  r = r - c2;
  r = r - c4;
  r = r - c6;
  r.set_output("y");
}
```

The mismatch is probably in the level-5 group: `p25` multiplies `t2` (level 2) by `t3` (level 3), so one operand is aligned and its scale differs from `t3sq` and `p34`. This version multiplies the coefficient into one factor first, giving `(t2*c5)*t3`, `(t3*c6)*t3` and `(t3*c7)*t4`. `A5` lands at level 4 and `A6`, `A7` at level 5, so the terms that share a level also share the same multiplication pattern. The `z*c` pieces and the constant subtractions stay as before.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");

  Ciphertext z2 = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = z2 + z2 - one;
  Ciphertext zt2 = z * t2;
  Ciphertext t3 = zt2 + zt2 - z;
  Ciphertext t2sq = square(t2);
  Ciphertext t4 = t2sq + t2sq - one;

  Ciphertext A5 = (t2 * c5) * t3;
  Ciphertext A6 = (t3 * c6) * t3;
  Ciphertext A7 = (t3 * c7) * t4;

  Ciphertext G3 = z2 * c2;
  Ciphertext G4 = zt2 * c3 + t2sq * c4;
  Ciphertext G5 = A6 + A7;

  Ciphertext s = G3 + G4;
  s = s + A5;
  s = s + G5;
  Ciphertext d = s + s;

  Ciphertext Zg = z * c1;
  Zg = Zg - z * c3;
  Zg = Zg - z * c5;
  Zg = Zg - z * c7;

  Ciphertext r = d + Zg;
  r = r + c0;
  r = r - c2;
  r = r - c4;
  r = r - c6;
  r.set_output("y");
}
```

The level-5 mismatches suggest that same-level terms with different histories are the problem in the deep products. This version uses the shallow identity only for `T4 = 2·T2² − 1` and keeps the baseline-style chain `z*t_{k-1}` for `T5`, `T6` and `T7`. That puts the terms `c5*t5`, `c6*t6`, `c7*t7` at levels 5, 6 and 7, all distinct. The only same-level pair is `c3*t3` and `c4*t4`, which share a history and are summed first. Everything is then added in ascending level order.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");

  Ciphertext z2 = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = z2 + z2 - one;
  Ciphertext zt2 = z * t2;
  Ciphertext t3 = zt2 + zt2 - z;
  Ciphertext t2sq = square(t2);
  Ciphertext t4 = t2sq + t2sq - one;
  Ciphertext zt4 = z * t4;
  Ciphertext t5 = zt4 + zt4 - t3;
  Ciphertext zt5 = z * t5;
  Ciphertext t6 = zt5 + zt5 - t4;
  Ciphertext zt6 = z * t6;
  Ciphertext t7 = zt6 + zt6 - t5;

  Ciphertext r = z * c1;
  r = r + t2 * c2;
  Ciphertext g = t3 * c3 + t4 * c4;
  r = r + g;
  r = r + t5 * c5;
  r = r + t6 * c6;
  r = r + t7 * c7;
  r = r + c0;
  r.set_output("y");
}
```

The level-4 mismatch was probably `t3*c3 + t4*c4`: `t3` and `t4` were built differently (one contains `-z`, the other `-one`). This version changes the construction so the one colliding pair has the same form:

- `t3 = 2·z·t2 − z`
- `t4 = 2·z·t3 − t2` and `t5 = 2·t2·t3 − z`, both a ciphertext product minus a lower ciphertext
- `t6 = 2·z·t5 − t4`
- `t7 = 2·z·t6 − t5`

Term levels are then 2, 3, 4, 5 (`t4` and `t5` together), 6 and 7.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");

  Ciphertext z2 = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = z2 + z2 - one;
  Ciphertext zt2 = z * t2;
  Ciphertext t3 = zt2 + zt2 - z;
  Ciphertext zt3 = z * t3;
  Ciphertext t4 = zt3 + zt3 - t2;
  Ciphertext p25 = t2 * t3;
  Ciphertext t5 = p25 + p25 - z;
  Ciphertext zt5 = z * t5;
  Ciphertext t6 = zt5 + zt5 - t4;
  Ciphertext zt6 = z * t6;
  Ciphertext t7 = zt6 + zt6 - t5;

  Ciphertext r = z * c1;
  r = r + t2 * c2;
  r = r + t3 * c3;
  Ciphertext g = t4 * c4 + t5 * c5;
  r = r + g;
  r = r + t6 * c6;
  r = r + t7 * c7;
  r = r + c0;
  r.set_output("y");
}
```

The remaining failure is likely `t4*c4 + t5*c5`. The lower operand that gets aligned is `t2` for `t4` but `z` for `t5`, so the sums have different alignment histories even at the same level. I couldn't find a shallower construction without such same-level collisions in the three attempts I have left.

For this attempt I'm going back to the baseline recurrence, which gives each term a distinct level (`c_k*t_k` at level k+1) and is known to compile. The only change is using `square` for `z*z`, so this is mainly a safe fallback rather than a real speed-up.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");

  vector<Ciphertext> t(8);
  t[1] = z;
  Ciphertext z_square = square(z);
  t[2] = z_square + z_square - one;
  for (int k = 3; k <= 7; ++k)
  {
    Ciphertext z_t = z * t[k - 1];
    t[k] = z_t + z_t - t[k - 2];
  }

  Ciphertext r = t[1] * c1;
  r = r + t[2] * c2;
  r = r + t[3] * c3;
  r = r + t[4] * c4;
  r = r + t[5] * c5;
  r = r + t[6] * c6;
  r = r + t[7] * c7;
  r = r + c0;
  r.set_output("y");
}
```

The best result so far is still the baseline (358.63 ms for the last attempt against 357.52 ms). For the last attempt I'm switching to Clenshaw evaluation of the Chebyshev series. The recurrence is b_k = c_k + 2·z·b_{k+1} − b_{k+2}, finishing with y = c0 + z·b1 − b2.

- **Multiplications:** this needs only 6 ciphertext–ciphertext multiplies (z·b6 … z·b1, plus the plaintext multiply z·c7) and none of the 8 coefficient multiplies on T_k.
- **Scale safety:** each b_k combines a product at the newest level with a ciphertext two levels lower and a plaintext. Nothing at the same level is added, so the scale mismatches I hit earlier should not occur.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");

  Ciphertext zc7 = z * c7;
  Ciphertext b6 = zc7 + zc7 + c6;

  Ciphertext p5 = z * b6;
  Ciphertext b5 = p5 + p5 + c5 - c7;

  Ciphertext p4 = z * b5;
  Ciphertext b4 = p4 + p4 + c4 - b6;

  Ciphertext p3 = z * b4;
  Ciphertext b3 = p3 + p3 + c3 - b5;

  Ciphertext p2 = z * b3;
  Ciphertext b2 = p2 + p2 + c2 - b4;

  Ciphertext p1 = z * b2;
  Ciphertext b1 = p1 + p1 + c1 - b3;

  Ciphertext p0 = z * b1;
  Ciphertext y = p0 + c0 - b2;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```
